In the test bicycle the steel frame (2.6 kg finished, 1.3 bought per kg) carries $2.2 \times 3.38 = 7.44$ kg CO₂e, while 1.9 kg of primary aluminium in rims and bars carries 30.4 kg: the lighter material dominates. The buy-to-finished ratio matters most for expensive-to-make materials machined from solid: aerospace titanium parts are often machined from billets with buy-to-fly ratios of 10 or more, which is one reason additive manufacturing of titanium can reduce embodied carbon despite its energy-hungry process.

